## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical problem solving for shared Linux runtimes. It focuses on beginner-friendly CPU isolation using cgroup v2 primitives rather than app changes.

## Key Concepts
- cgroup v2 CPU controller (cpu.max, cpu.weight)
- systemd slices or cpuset for per-tenant isolation
- basic benchmarking of latency under bursts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Reads current cpu.max for a given cgroup path
const fs = require('fs');
try {
  const quota = fs.readFileSync('/sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.slice/cpu.max','utf8').trim();
  console.log('cpu.max:', quota);
} catch (e) { console.error(e); }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this plan to handle bursty tenants with varying baselines?
- What risks exist with aggressive quotas and how would you detect them?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical mastery of Linux memory isolation, NUMA binding, and low-latency multitenant workloads under fragmentation pressure.

## Key Concepts
- memcg v2 quotas, cpuset, NUMA bindings
- HugePages/THP strategy; allocator isolation (jemalloc arenas)
- kernel memory.compaction tuning; vmscan knobs
- per-tenant BPF observability; staged rollouts

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal eBPF: count major page allocations per cgroup id
#include <uapi/linux/ptrace.h>
BPF_HASH(page_allocs, u64, u64);
int trace_alloc(struct trace_event_raw_alloc *e) {
  u64 cid = bpf_get_current_uid_gid();
  page_allocs.increment(cid);
  return 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify fragmentation impact and rollback criteria?
- Which tenants get priority during compaction?

## Why This Is Asked

This situation tests ability to design robust multi-tenant resource governance with BPF maps, a common source of memory leaks in real-world deployments.

## Key Concepts

- BPF map accounting per tenant
- Map lifecycle and eviction strategies
- Namespace/isolation boundaries
- Observability with bpftool, perf, pidstat
- Canary rollouts and rollback plans

## Code Example

```c
